[Music]  parents across the country know  firsthand how the pandemic has been  affecting their children's mental health  now first on cbs morning's a new poll  from the child advocacy advocacy group  are on our sleeves shows how those  challenges are affecting parents in the  workplace the survey found that 53  percent have missed work at least once a  month to deal with their children's  mental health and 54 percent have  interrupted their work day at least one  once a month to address a child's mental  health needs marty bletzel post is  executive director of on our sleeves  good morning how are you doing good  morning thanks for having me so what did  the survey reveal about the impact of  children's mental health on parents  well even when parents are at work 30 to  50 percent of their mind share can be on  worrying about whether their child's  mental health is developing as it should  and whether everything's okay so yes  parents are missing work and  interrupting work and even when they're  at work this is on their mind all the  time so 41 of parents surveyed said that  their focus and their concern on their  children's mental health increased  during the pandemic yes um why is that  so and do you believe that once the the  covet rates decline and restrictions  lift that these things will be resolved  it's hard for us to predict the future  but our behavioral health experts are  looking at the past 10-year trends and  seeing that children's mental health  issues are rising and parents are more  and more concerned so if we look back to  look forward we have to be ready for new  adjustments as life after the pandemic  continues to change and we have to be  ready to adjust with it and adjust for  our kids and help them have  conversations about their mental health  with everything that's coming at them so  what about parents that work from home  like how has that impacted their  children's mental health and then also  theirs well we asked parents how  disruptive has your child's mental  health been and how disruptive has  remote schooling been and they were only  four percentage points apart so we know  that it's been very difficult in both of  those scenarios to stay focused and get  work done when all this other  concern is coming at them and i can  assume that there's a correlation but  working from home and then also  doing two things at once being a teacher  as well that has to be difficult on  parents right right parents are used to  multitasking but the last couple years  have been extra extra multitasking  effort now what do parents have  available  for  ways to help them deal with their  children's mental health situation the  first thing we can all do is start to  break down the stigma around this topic  half of all lifetime mental illness will  present before the age of 14.  so children have mental health needs  just like adults so if we can start to  have this conversation with each other  with coaches with counselors with  teachers and in the workplace then  employers can join us in finding  solutions and helping their employees be  more focused and productive when they're  on the job we've been talking about  removing the stigma  surrounding  mental health for a long time and like  you said it is difficult in the  workplace so what kind of advice do you  give to parents that have to deal with  that because sometimes you you don't  want to say anything right to your  friends to your family members and  really to your co-workers right we asked  parents about that and coworkers and hr  and colleagues they came in at the  bottom of the list of adults that  parents want to talk to  but 85 percent of parents said they want  to talk to another adult they feel like  that would be helpful and we know that  workplaces can connect them to resources  so we suggest that you start small find  one other parent in the workplace that  you can trust  ask hr some really general questions  about what benefits you have available  what resources are there without  mentioning a specific concern or  diagnosis so that you can get a sense of  what you can do and then start to think  about how do you build effort inside the  workplace and be more transparent and  say things like i need to take a mental  health day with my kid or i need to be  present with my child through something  that's really you know impacting him or  her and start to have these phrases come  out so that we can all just break that  stigma um communicating that helps marty  bledsoe post thank you so much i  appreciate you joining us if you feel  like your child is in danger of hurting  themselves contact the national suicide  prevention lifeline at  800  800-273-8255

